WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:07.202
Code models can generate information-rich visual artifacts
that are editable, verifiable, and logically structured.

2
00:00:07.563 --> 00:00:11.254
Yet the results often suffer from poor or dated visual design.

3
00:00:11.778 --> 00:00:17.117
AesCode draws on a reference image generated from
the same prompt to provide aesthetic guidance.

4
00:00:17.708 --> 00:00:26.205
AesCode generates information-rich posters, slides,
dashboards, and webpages as editable HTML and CSS.

5
00:00:26.729 --> 00:00:32.985
Our eight-billion or thirty-two-billion parameter models produce
visual results comparable to closed-source frontier models.

6
00:00:33.598 --> 00:00:35.826
Here are some examples generated by AesCode.

7
00:00:36.187 --> 00:00:42.780
The model adapts layout, hierarchy, spacing,
and color to each brief, while keeping the output editable as code.

8
00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:20.882
How do we learn from that guidance without copying its mistakes?

9
00:01:21.244 --> 00:01:28.208
We model the design target explicitly as a graph,
derive complementary rewards, and use them to train the code policy.

10
00:01:28.865 --> 00:01:35.853
This design graph connects the whole page to its details.
Nodes represent elements such as titles, charts, and cards.

11
00:01:36.284 --> 00:01:41.461
Attributes describe their content and geometry,
while edges capture relationships.

12
00:01:41.984 --> 00:01:45.350
Each check can now target a specific design property.

13
00:01:45.842 --> 00:01:49.521
From this graph, there are two complementary verification paths.

14
00:01:49.766 --> 00:01:52.554
Deterministic verifiers inspect code and rendering

15
00:01:52.564 --> 00:01:57.741
for execution, text, boundaries, data, layout, and whitespace.

16
00:01:58.044 --> 00:02:01.491
The Visual Graph Rubric handles the remaining visual properties.

17
00:02:01.736 --> 00:02:07.644
It uses each sample's graph to anchor yes-or-no
questions to elements and relationships.

18
00:02:08.168 --> 00:02:13.402
A vision-language judge answers from the candidate
rendering, providing targeted design feedback.

19
00:02:14.086 --> 00:02:20.227
We combine these signals with GDPO, normalizing each
reward channel separately before updating the policy.

20
00:02:20.865 --> 00:02:22.292
Here are the key findings.

21
00:02:22.653 --> 00:02:28.468
Reference images help at inference, and image-based
supervision builds design capability during training.

22
00:02:28.992 --> 00:02:35.701
Our compact models achieve competitive visual quality.
Style remains a shared bottleneck, below 60%.

23
00:02:36.331 --> 00:02:42.680
These qualitative comparisons show why closed-source
frontier models can lag in specific dimensions:

24
00:02:43.204 --> 00:02:48.462
overlapping headlines, missing required content,
and charts obscured by other elements.

25
00:02:49.108 --> 00:02:53.832
Still, limited model capacity may affect
icon detail and complex relationships,

26
00:02:54.193 --> 00:02:57.593
while hallucinated reference content can also introduce errors.
